Reasoning:
- **Shared trunks**: The incumbent pays each edge once per destination (× num_partitions). The Steiner tree merges shared prefixes, so costly egress edges from `src` are paid once and fan out at cheap downstream nodes — the exact gain mechanism the per-dst Dijkstra cannot represent.
- **None-cost handling**: Unpriced edges (reported by `make_nx_graph`) are treated as 0 in the tree construction, and every appended hop is verified to be a real directed edge of `h`, so output format and validity are preserved.
- **Fallback**: Per-dst Dijkstra (byte-identical appending logic to the incumbent) runs only when the tree route is missing/incomplete, so worst case matches the incumbent's cost rather than regressing.
- **Budget**: One `steiner_tree` call plus a BFS expansion — well within the 600s limit.
- This is structurally different from the previously explored per-dst free-link variant (scores 0.000965/0.000945/0.000924), avoiding re-derivation.
